        "com.apple.security.application-groups": {
          "status": "prohibited",
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule G4): no grant this entitlement confers on macOS is used by the selected design. The six grants were read out of the system App Sandbox profile rather than assumed - three are home-relative and break across the root/user boundary, three are system-wide and belong to mechanisms this project does not use (Mac App Store group content, a group-prefixed XPC Mach service, group-prefixed POSIX IPC). The host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and its XPC candidate - the only one that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name - was rejected. A private group container is redundant with the container every sandboxed target already gets unconditionally (application.sb:107-108).",
          "evidence": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): the entitlement appears at lines 302-330 and 459-465 and nowhere else. Full per-grant survey in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.survey.",
          "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
