    "driftPolicy": "fail-closed",
    "assertionScopeContract": {
      "id": "S2",
      "statement": "An assertion carries NO independent authority over WHICH targets or profiles it applies to. Every scope an assertion states is DERIVED from the authoritative rows — the target rows for an entitlement claim, the profile rows for a profile claim — and rules R35 and R36 recompute the derivation three ways and compare it against the assertion's own words before the contract is allowed to pass. An assertion may not claim a target it does not hold, may not omit one it does, and may not name a target at all unless the mention is registered below.",
      "history": "Reviewer verdict 07. r6 closed exactly this defect class for the cross-platform summary (rule S1) and left the assertion list — the OTHER prose projection of the same rows — unchecked. Two assertions had already gone stale under it. A5 said application-groups is present on every iOS bundle, which r5 had made false for the iOS probe pair and which A18 directly contradicted; following A5 would have over-entitled both disposable probes. A9 said every development profile carries this Mac, which is false for the four iOS development profiles whose devices are deferred under ADR-024. The 1024-check r7 validator passed because R27 checked assertion MEMBERSHIP and substrings, never semantic scope against the rows. A third instance was found while fixing these two and is recorded in staleClaimsFound.",
      "mechanism": "Each scope-bearing assertion has one entry here per polarity. Each entry declares classPredicates over the authoritative rows, and the SET those predicates select must equal the entry's declared scopeTargets, which must in turn equal the set the row STATUSES imply for that entitlement key and polarity. Three independent derivations of the same set: a predicate typo, a stale hand-written list, and a moved row each break a different one. The entry's scopeClause must then appear VERBATIM in the assertion text and again in the rationale document, and every target named anywhere in the assertion text must be either inside that assertion's scope or a declared nonScopeMention with its own clause present.",
      "polarityNote": "present — the clause asserts the key is present on scopeTargets. absent — the clause asserts it is absent from scopeTargets. present-exclusive — the clause asserts presence on scopeTargets AND, through an explicit exclusivityMarker that must appear in the text, absence everywhere else; it therefore discharges the complement on its own and needs no paired absent entry. Choosing present-exclusive without the marker in the text is a failure, because 'present on X' alone says nothing about the rest.",
      "partitionRule": "For every entitlement key registered here, the union of the present scopes must equal the set of target rows whose status for that key is an authored status, the union of the absent scopes (plus the complement discharged by any present-exclusive entry) must equal every remaining target row, and the two must be disjoint. A5 and A18 failed exactly this: their scopes OVERLAPPED on the two iOS probe rows, one claiming presence and the other absence, and nothing computed the union.",
      "coverageRule": "Every entitlement key that appears literally in ANY assertion text must have at least one entry here, and every registered key must appear in the text of the assertion it is registered against. That is what makes the class fail closed rather than this one pair: a future assertion that names an entitlement key without registering its scope cannot pass R35, and a registered scope whose assertion stopped mentioning the key cannot either.",
      "profileScopeRule": "A profile assertion is scoped the same way, over profiles[] rather than targets[]. deviceBinding declares what the profile rows must say about devices for the scope to be honest: 'enumerated' requires a concrete device list on every profile in scope, 'deferred' requires every device entry on every profile in scope to carry both the deferral marker and the deferral basis, and 'all-devices' is the distribution-channel form that provisions every device instead of listing any. The development-channel entries must partition the profile rows, and the enumerated set must equal the set of profiles Ceremony C1 authorizes — which is the tie that keeps A9a and the C1 scope from drifting apart.",
      "deferredDeviceMarker": "deferred",
      "deferralBasis": "ADR-024",
      "deferredMarkerNote": "A device entry is deferred if and only if it contains BOTH strings. Requiring the basis as well as the word means a device set cannot be quietly parked without naming the decision that parked it.",
      "authorityNote": "scopeClause is normative and machine-checked in both artifacts. The remaining prose in an assertion is explanatory and carries no scope: it may not introduce a target that is neither in scope nor a declared mention, which is the only way explanatory prose could smuggle a scope back in.",
      "staleClaimsFound": [
        {
          "where": "verification.assertions[A5]",
          "reportedBy": "reviewer verdict 07 F1",
          "wrongSince": "2026-07-28.r5",
          "claim": "application-groups is present on every iOS bundle",
          "truth": "r5 prohibited it on both iOS probe rows under rule G4's probeRule",
          "consequence": "an entitlement check following A5 would over-entitle both probes"
        },
        {
          "where": "verification.assertions[A9]",
          "reportedBy": "reviewer verdict 07 F2",
          "wrongSince": "2026-07-28.r1",
          "claim": "every development profile carries ProvisionedDevices containing this Mac",
          "truth": "the four iOS development profiles declare devices deferred under ADR-024",
          "consequence": "a profile check following A9 would fail every iOS development profile the moment one existed, or would silently pass by treating the deferral sentinel as a device name"
        },
        {
          "where": "the rationale document's assertion table, row A6",
          "reportedBy": "found by this revision while registering A6's scope, not reported by any verdict",
          "wrongSince": "2026-07-28.r5",
          "claim": "keychain-access-groups present where status is required — ios.host, ios.provider, macos.host; absent from macos.provider and the whole probe family",
          "truth": "r5 removed the group from macos.host under rule K2, so the grant set is ios.host and ios.provider alone and every macOS row is prohibited",
          "consequence": "the prose artifact still granted the macOS host a keychain group the JSON prohibits — the verdict-05 F1 defect, one revision later and one file over. R33 did not catch it because R33 renders grantClauses, not assertions; R35's rationale-document check is what closes that half."
        }
